    ($SATQ$0aebdf5f$SATQ$, $SATQ$c93d96b4-b4f6-4fbb-ac6f-e2d30b6c9ef6$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>According to linguist Martin Joos, speakers of the English language <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> five main registers&mdash;frozen, formal, consultative, casual, and intimate&mdash;which they rotate between depending on the situation.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"46a2306d-2f6a-4e86-a6a2-14c0753e371a","content":"<p>use</p>"},{"id":"7354e105-a80b-4c31-845d-0db1a32fe246","content":"<p>is using</p>"},{"id":"0ee9fe19-4e84-4dae-92a1-66ee2746e5b1","content":"<p>uses</p>"},{"id":"d68df954-d386-4e52-aac4-1193d37f03f2","content":"<p>has used</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["46a2306d-2f6a-4e86-a6a2-14c0753e371a"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The plural verb "use" agrees in number with the plural subject "speakers."</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the singular verb "is using" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural subject "speakers." Choice C is incorrect because the singular verb "uses" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural subject "speakers." Choice D is incorrect because the singular verb "has used" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural subject "speakers."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0aebdf5f","external_id":"c93d96b4-b4f6-4fbb-ac6f-e2d30b6c9ef6","vaultid":null,"uId":"25f281cf-80e9-4a12-9358-85b3113dc8f2","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>According to linguist Martin Joos, speakers of the English language <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> five main registers&mdash;frozen, formal, consultative, casual, and intimate&mdash;which they rotate between depending on the situation.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"46a2306d-2f6a-4e86-a6a2-14c0753e371a","content":"<p>use</p>"},{"id":"7354e105-a80b-4c31-845d-0db1a32fe246","content":"<p>is using</p>"},{"id":"0ee9fe19-4e84-4dae-92a1-66ee2746e5b1","content":"<p>uses</p>"},{"id":"d68df954-d386-4e52-aac4-1193d37f03f2","content":"<p>has used</p>"}],"keys":["46a2306d-2f6a-4e86-a6a2-14c0753e371a"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The plural verb \"use\" agrees in number with the plural subject \"speakers.\"</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the singular verb \"is using\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural subject \"speakers.\" Choice C is incorrect because the singular verb \"uses\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural subject \"speakers.\" Choice D is incorrect because the singular verb \"has used\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural subject \"speakers.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323051,"updateDate":1730147323051}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323051, 1730147323051),
    ($SATQ$0b005ae2$SATQ$, $SATQ$d2dee6cb-c775-4f13-b8cd-a1e34eb70c1d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Recently unearthed Neronian tools in France dating to 54,000 years ago and attributed to <em>Homo sapiens</em>&nbsp;may provide evidence that interactions between Neanderthals and modern humans occurred 10,000 years earlier than was previously <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> finding that, if true, would overturn current theories about <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias="H">H.</span> sapiens</em> migration during the Upper Paleolithic.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"aaf62559-6640-4c0f-8969-2c62f29f96cb","content":"<p>supposed<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span> a</p>"},{"id":"284d115f-5d4d-45f5-a094-1b99c4514e7f","content":"<p>supposed<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span> A</p>"},{"id":"96eb3982-30b5-4a2c-9b21-ff6fac9faef2","content":"<p>supposed a</p>"},{"id":"c2f33dc1-7204-41fa-96d7-bfc17148450b","content":"<p>supposed<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> a</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["c2f33dc1-7204-41fa-96d7-bfc17148450b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. This choice correctly uses a comma to mark the boundary between the main clause ("Recently...supposed") and the supplementary element ("a finding...Paleolithic") that provides additional information about the implications of the Neronian tool discovery.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because a semicolon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join the main clause ("Recently...supposed") and the supplementary element ("a finding...Paleolithic"). Choice B is incorrect because it results in a rhetorically unacceptable sentence fragment beginning with "a finding." Choice C is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The main clause ("Recently...supposed") and the supplementary element ("a finding...Paleolithic") are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0b005ae2","external_id":"d2dee6cb-c775-4f13-b8cd-a1e34eb70c1d","vaultid":null,"uId":"e271237b-5426-473b-8c8c-df7bf61de692","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Recently unearthed Neronian tools in France dating to 54,000 years ago and attributed to <em>Homo sapiens</em>&nbsp;may provide evidence that interactions between Neanderthals and modern humans occurred 10,000 years earlier than was previously <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> finding that, if true, would overturn current theories about <em><span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"H\">H.</span> sapiens</em> migration during the Upper Paleolithic.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"aaf62559-6640-4c0f-8969-2c62f29f96cb","content":"<p>supposed<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">;</span> a</p>"},{"id":"284d115f-5d4d-45f5-a094-1b99c4514e7f","content":"<p>supposed<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">.</span> A</p>"},{"id":"96eb3982-30b5-4a2c-9b21-ff6fac9faef2","content":"<p>supposed a</p>"},{"id":"c2f33dc1-7204-41fa-96d7-bfc17148450b","content":"<p>supposed<span data-ssml-say-as=\"characters\">,</span> a</p>"}],"keys":["c2f33dc1-7204-41fa-96d7-bfc17148450b"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of punctuation within a sentence. This choice correctly uses a comma to mark the boundary between the main clause (\"Recently...supposed\") and the supplementary element (\"a finding...Paleolithic\") that provides additional information about the implications of the Neronian tool discovery.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because a semicolon can&rsquo;t be used in this way to join the main clause (\"Recently...supposed\") and the supplementary element (\"a finding...Paleolithic\"). Choice B is incorrect because it results in a rhetorically unacceptable sentence fragment beginning with \"a finding.\" Choice C is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The main clause (\"Recently...supposed\") and the supplementary element (\"a finding...Paleolithic\") are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669213,"updateDate":1743430669213}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669213, 1743430669213),
    ($SATQ$0bcb4417$SATQ$, $SATQ$57bf2f17-e513-4511-bd95-5bce2f8866c2$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$4d0070ad-9430-4d5c-b7b4-b61e959c9cb5$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Oglala Lakota poet Layli Long Soldier&rsquo;s star quilt poems offer an unusually open-ended reading experience. With <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> eight panels of text stitched together in the shape of a traditional eight-pointed Lakota star quilt, the poems present viewers with a seemingly infinite number of ways to read them.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4fcfe390-c259-40a0-87b1-cbdef2024a28","content":"<p>their</p>"},{"id":"7a2be220-e638-463b-85e5-4a28099f777a","content":"<p>it&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"c662a3da-2e66-480c-9d0c-b50a7352c5d9","content":"<p>they&rsquo;re</p>"},{"id":"8c51f4e7-b33d-444e-bf78-fd72542cbb83","content":"<p>its</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4fcfe390-c259-40a0-87b1-cbdef2024a28"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of possessive determiners. The plural possessive determiner "their" agrees in number with the plural noun "the poems," thus indicating that the poems had eight panels of text stitched together in the shape of a traditional eight-pointed Lakota star quilt.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because "it&rsquo;s" is the contraction for "it is" or "it has," not a possessive determiner. Choice C is incorrect because "they&rsquo;re" is the contraction for "they are," not a possessive determiner. Choice D is incorrect because the singular possessive determiner "its" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural noun "the poems."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-FSS-M006$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$6b6bbc78-4e80-4302-8094-a3ae55c34e6e$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0bcb4417","external_id":"57bf2f17-e513-4511-bd95-5bce2f8866c2","vaultid":"4d0070ad-9430-4d5c-b7b4-b61e959c9cb5","uId":"bdf5ac1e-e076-48d1-be2f-626ca84461f0","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"FSS","skill_desc":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Oglala Lakota poet Layli Long Soldier&rsquo;s star quilt poems offer an unusually open-ended reading experience. With <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> eight panels of text stitched together in the shape of a traditional eight-pointed Lakota star quilt, the poems present viewers with a seemingly infinite number of ways to read them.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4fcfe390-c259-40a0-87b1-cbdef2024a28","content":"<p>their</p>"},{"id":"7a2be220-e638-463b-85e5-4a28099f777a","content":"<p>it&rsquo;s</p>"},{"id":"c662a3da-2e66-480c-9d0c-b50a7352c5d9","content":"<p>they&rsquo;re</p>"},{"id":"8c51f4e7-b33d-444e-bf78-fd72542cbb83","content":"<p>its</p>"}],"keys":["4fcfe390-c259-40a0-87b1-cbdef2024a28"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is the use of possessive determiners. The plural possessive determiner \"their\" agrees in number with the plural noun \"the poems,\" thus indicating that the poems had eight panels of text stitched together in the shape of a traditional eight-pointed Lakota star quilt.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because \"it&rsquo;s\" is the contraction for \"it is\" or \"it has,\" not a possessive determiner. Choice C is incorrect because \"they&rsquo;re\" is the contraction for \"they are,\" not a possessive determiner. Choice D is incorrect because the singular possessive determiner \"its\" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the plural noun \"the poems.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-FSS-M006","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"6b6bbc78-4e80-4302-8094-a3ae55c34e6e","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959367,"updateDate":1691007959367}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959367, 1691007959367),
    ($SATQ$0f39b19c$SATQ$, $SATQ$78f6a3d3-014c-4f0b-b669-c873155cf2c3$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$9ca87d98-beaf-4c4f-abc8-00581341a3a8$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$BOU$SATQ$, $SATQ$Boundaries$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>After a spate of illnesses as a child, Wilma Rudolph was told she might never walk again. Defying all odds, Rudolph didn&rsquo;t just walk, she <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the 1960 Summer Olympics in Rome, she won both the 100- and 200-meter dashes and clinched first place for her team in the 4x100-meter relay, becoming the first US woman to win three gold medals in a single Olympics. </p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b6f4071a-be0a-4cef-99c1-e17f1dca1cf7","content":"<p>ran&mdash;fast&mdash;during</p>"},{"id":"ddd90ce0-b593-437b-a242-7346795e76da","content":"<p>ran&mdash;fast during</p>"},{"id":"6388f221-0ee1-4841-8ace-f89160c297e2","content":"<p>ran&mdash;fast, during</p>"},{"id":"5833d375-9d18-443a-91ea-043c5e3e5954","content":"<p>ran&mdash;fast. During</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["5833d375-9d18-443a-91ea-043c5e3e5954"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The convention being tested is punctuation use between sentences. In this choice, the period is used correctly to mark the boundary between one sentence (&ldquo;Defying&hellip;fast&rdquo;) and another sentence that begins with a supplementary phrase (&ldquo;During&hellip;Olympics&rdquo;). </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. When a dash is present in a sentence (&ldquo;ran&mdash;fast&rdquo;), it&rsquo;s not conventional to use another dash (&ldquo;fast&mdash;during&rdquo;) to mark the boundary between sentences because it creates a potentially confusing sentence. In this context, a period, semicolon, or colon would be clear and more conventional. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a run-on sentence. The sentences (&ldquo;Defying&hellip;fast&rdquo;) and (&ldquo;during&hellip;Olympics&rdquo;) are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between sentences. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-641-SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$bec437e0-e640-4c34-ae5c-8b70f2e54f45$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"0f39b19c","external_id":"78f6a3d3-014c-4f0b-b669-c873155cf2c3","vaultid":"9ca87d98-beaf-4c4f-abc8-00581341a3a8","uId":"142a32df-2b88-48c8-8980-1ee6294b037d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"SEC","domain":"Standard English Conventions","skill_cd":"BOU","skill_desc":"Boundaries","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>After a spate of illnesses as a child, Wilma Rudolph was told she might never walk again. Defying all odds, Rudolph didn&rsquo;t just walk, she <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the 1960 Summer Olympics in Rome, she won both the 100- and 200-meter dashes and clinched first place for her team in the 4x100-meter relay, becoming the first US woman to win three gold medals in a single Olympics. </p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b6f4071a-be0a-4cef-99c1-e17f1dca1cf7","content":"<p>ran&mdash;fast&mdash;during</p>"},{"id":"ddd90ce0-b593-437b-a242-7346795e76da","content":"<p>ran&mdash;fast during</p>"},{"id":"6388f221-0ee1-4841-8ace-f89160c297e2","content":"<p>ran&mdash;fast, during</p>"},{"id":"5833d375-9d18-443a-91ea-043c5e3e5954","content":"<p>ran&mdash;fast. During</p>"}],"keys":["5833d375-9d18-443a-91ea-043c5e3e5954"],"correc