ce. The sentences are fused without punctuation and/or a conjunction. Choice B is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between sentences. Choice C is incorrect because it results in a comma splice. A comma can&rsquo;t be used in this way to mark the boundary between sentences.</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-740-SEC","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"f9be6c81-e45a-4f22-99fe-f9bded3ff9ea","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959350,"updateDate":1691007959350}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959350, 1691007959350),
    ($SATQ$0fe5ce68$SATQ$, $SATQ$05b16bae-aa49-4296-b74b-56a697e560b0$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$7965e1f1-cfff-4cb5-b31c-a394248bb344$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Standard English Conventions$SATQ$, $SATQ$SEC$SATQ$, $SATQ$FSS$SATQ$, $SATQ$Form, Structure, and Sense$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Ten of William Shakespeare&rsquo;s plays are classified as histories. Although each one of these plays, which include <em>Henry V</em> and <em>Richard III</em>, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> on a single historical figure (specifically, an English king), some, such as <em>Henry VI Part One</em> and <em>Henry VI Part Two, </em>feature different episodes from the same monarch&rsquo;s life.&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"09ddd0ce-6205-4676-b7e0-f0564a201a74","content":"<p>focuses</p>"},{"id":"7546896c-d6a8-4292-9d80-5a721d2b4f65","content":"<p>focus</p>"},{"id":"3142f551-8dca-46a2-9038-adc619085cd3","content":"<p>are focused</p>"},{"id":"9bfbdcb8-c5c3-46a7-8e32-83213d3d17f9","content":"<p>were focused</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["09ddd0ce-6205-4676-b7e0-f0564a201a74"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The convention being tested is subject-verb agreement. The singular verb "focuses" agrees in number with the singular subject "each one of these plays," which refers to each play individually.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because the plural verb "focus" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "each one of these plays." Choice C is incorrect because the plural verb "are focused" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject "each one of these plays." Choice D is incorrect because the plural verb "were focused" doesn&rsquo;t agree in number with the singular subject 